WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.000
ED GLAESER: Some urban problems, like sky high housing pricing,

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:05.350
are mostly a curse in wealthy cities.

00:00:05.350 --> 00:00:08.405
Other urban scourges, like filthy water and contagious disease,

00:00:08.405 --> 00:00:11.202
primarily impact the world's poorest metropolitan areas.

00:00:11.202 --> 00:00:13.750
But traffic congestion is universal.

00:00:13.750 --> 00:00:16.358
It is a challenge in London and in Dar al Salam.

00:00:16.358 --> 00:00:19.501
Any time there are a lot of people using a small amount of space,

00:00:19.501 --> 00:00:23.580
which is to say any time you have a city, mobility is going to be a challenge.

00:00:23.580 --> 00:00:27.700
The ubiquitous problem of urban immobility may be a bit surprising.

00:00:27.700 --> 00:00:31.005
After all, urban density's ultimate benefit is to make it easier for

00:00:31.005 --> 00:00:34.000
people to physically connect with one another.

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:36.980
Yet that is exactly why congestion is so important.

00:00:36.980 --> 00:00:40.110
It strikes at the most important function of urban density.

00:00:40.110 --> 00:00:43.280
You shouldn't expect this course to help you negotiate your morning commute any

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:46.140
easier given the current state of transportation in your city.

00:00:46.140 --> 00:00:47.345
I figure you've got that one covered.

00:00:47.345 --> 00:00:51.775
We're going to focus on strategies to make the city as a whole more mobile.

00:00:51.775 --> 00:00:54.055
And that could only help improve your morning commute,

00:00:54.055 --> 00:00:57.410
If you help in the fight to improve transportation planning for your city.

00:00:57.410 --> 00:00:59.390
Driving remains the norm in American cities.

00:00:59.390 --> 00:01:02.490
And so we are gonna talk about building more roads and implementing

00:01:02.490 --> 00:01:06.385
other policies like congestion pricing that make existing roads more functional.

00:01:06.385 --> 00:01:09.720
We're also gonna focus on the glories of the underappreciated bus.

00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:12.390
Which can provide cheap and flexible transportation,

00:01:12.390 --> 00:01:15.610
that is particularly well suited for poorer medium density areas.

00:01:15.610 --> 00:01:18.260
Naturally, we'll also say something about trains as well.

00:01:18.260 --> 00:01:22.790
Metro systems often seem to provide an endo skeleton for global cities.

00:01:22.790 --> 00:01:25.400
We want to ask which investments make the most sense?

00:01:25.400 --> 00:01:27.720
And how to get most out of those investments.

00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:30.990
We're gonna talk a bit about what often goes wrong in transportation spending,

00:01:30.990 --> 00:01:34.080
including high costs in corruption and how to reduce those costs.

00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:37.750
Mobility is particularly tough for the world's poorest urbanites.

00:01:37.750 --> 00:01:39.361
Who, in cities like Sao Paulo,

00:01:39.361 --> 00:01:42.355
sometimes spend over three hours a day is stuck in traffic.

00:01:42.355 --> 00:01:46.244
So, better transportation isn't just about saving you or me a few minutes each day.

00:01:46.244 --> 00:01:49.651
But, it's also about ensuring that cities provides access to jobs for

00:01:49.651 --> 00:01:51.460
the most vulnerable urbanites.

